\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Ezekiel chapter 25 opens with something familiar from many earlier chapters of the book. Ezekiel is told to set his face. But the opponent, the target, is different now. Before, Jerusalem, or the land, or the people were whom the LORD opposed through Ezekiel. But after the oracles of chapter 24, the message is different. Now, his message is against the Ammonites.\
In the same way that chapter 24 also ended the entire section of Ezekiel in judgement on Israel, this setting of the face is, in a way, turning Ezekiel towards judgement on the nations.\
In light of what we have learned reading all the previous chapters of Ezekiel up until now, especially about Ezekiel's audience, Ezekiel himself, and the character and purposes of God, what does this oracle against Ammon demand of us? What else, beyond the opening of the oracle, has come again from the previous chapters, but possibly in a new way? How does this oracle speak to its original audience - quite possibly not the grammatical audience - and to us?
