I think we ought to sift through what we can of the evidence in this oracle first, starting with what you brought up.\
In chapter 36, the nation accused of that contempt is Edom.\'a0 Which, I think, only strengthens your case.\'a0 And chapter 25 verse 15 also uses the word similarly.\'a0 Edom and Philistia both destroy out of contempt.\'a0 Chapter 16 is not quite an accusation though - Philistines and Syrians and the surrounding peoples have contempt on Jerusalem, but this is presented as a punishment on Jerusalem for its lewdness and abominations, that is, its addictive idolatry.\'a0 Whether contempt was right or wrong on the part of the Philistines or Syrians is not addressed; the point is that Jerusalem became an object of reproach.\
But if we combine chapters 25 and 36 with chapter 16, we find something similar to what you observed with 'my sanctuary'.\'a0 The low state of Jerusalem was the LORD's doing, and even the contempt was a punishment.\'a0 But that same contempt - and from the same actor in the case of the Philistines - is blameworthy.\
What does that tell us of the message to the exiles?\'a0 Though they no longer live in Jerusalem, having come from there, the shame heaped on Jerusalem may impact them all the more now that they are physically safer.\'a0 And yet, their concern for the temple and their home city may be different in key ways from the LORD's zeal for his temple and for Jerusalem, the city where he had chosen to make his name dwell.\'a0 And even more so, their concern for the LORD's own name, and their understanding of what he will do for the same of his name, may be different.\'a0 Grammatically, then you will know that I am the LORD is directed at Ammon, and indeed, the phrase shows the LORD's concern that his destruction of his sanctuary and the exile of his people not be misinterpreted by the nations.\'a0 But the LORD may also intend for his people to know him through these actions, predicting them in advance that the intent might be understood.
