I may have identified something true, and you may have correctly followed that up.\'a0 It seems to me that you have. But while this evidence from other chapters can very much support a theory, only to the degree that theory has evidence within chapter 25 should we be confident in it.\'a0 This is difficult, because chapter 25 mentions God's people only as targets of unjust acts.\'a0 \
Lacking the evidence we need, one way or another, we go to the text anew; taking the text seriously means trusting that the text itself contains evidence we haven't seen yet. That is to say, I have not presented enough evidence for what i have claimed, and I need to open Ezekiel again.\
Here is what I find when I do so:\
We know that this text is pronouncing judgement on these nations.\'a0 But if we allow ourselves to be caught by the text, as if it were speaking to us, we hear something beyond that specific judgement.\'a0 The LORD is outraged at their behavior.\'a0 And part of that outrage is, as you pointed out with respect to the sanctuary and with respect to the house of Judah being like the nations, according to their failure to recognize the LORD's covenant care for his people and his reliability in keeping covenant.\'a0 The response to Edom does seem to communicate the LORD's covenant care for his people in how passionately he avenges his people, and the LORD is concerned that all know his vengeance in that matter, but if there are signs of this thinking the house of Judah is as any other nation, I do not see them.\'a0 The response to the Philistines may be just slightly a middle ground, in that it uses the word contempt, and it concludes by saying that they will know the LORD is the LORD.\
But perhaps you see more evidence in chapter 25, or see this evidence differently than I do.\
I have two more connections, outside chapter 25, which might be relevant.\
This may be evidence for or against your theory, but I think it mostly pushes orthogonally;\'a0 Ezekiel 5 tells us that the LORD placed Jerusalem in the center of the nations, with nations all around her, and Ezekiel 25 tells us of nations all around, moving in somewhat of a circle.\'a0 Ezekiel 5 may have been noting the mission of Jerusalem, the opportunity to make the LORD known made possible by being thus surrounded.\'a0 Ezekiel 5 definitely notes that Jersualem is failing even by the standards of the nations.\'a0 While God's standards are likely in view, the nations' standards may be as well in the last two oracles of the chapter.\
But of standards mentioned earlier in the book, it is chapter 20's standards that may be most relevant in chapter 25.\'a0 While other chapters imply that the LORD will act for the sake of his name, chapter 20 gives both explicit abstract statements and examples.
